  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="1.8179717682436798" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noaoann09014a&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2009-09-23T00:00:00+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="CYG" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="44.37097085158541" Magnitude="0" Name="IC 5067, Pelican Nebula" Opacity="100" RA="20.84877445330506" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noaoann09014a/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="1.8179717682436798">
    <Description>IC 5067, a star forming region in Cygnus, is also sometimes called the Pelican Nebula. This image was taken May 24, 2009 by Gregor Rothfuss, a first time visitor to the Kitt Peak Advanced Observing Program, with the help of Kitt Peak Visitor Center operator Flynn Haase. They used the VC 20-inch RC telescope with an SBIG STL-6303E camera and AstroDon LRGB filters. Exposures were 120:40:40:40 respectively. This was Mr. Rothfuss’s first experience doing CCD imaging. The Kitt Peak Visitor Center program, which receives no financial support from the observatory, offers paying guests the opportunity to do their own observing and imaging.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.45354308737296634" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="312.75055624732016" CenterY="44.21564058113938" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="IC 5067, Pelican Nebula" OffsetX="0.13502626500469894" OffsetY="-0.07886248133627202" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="115.09144338378539" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="2" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noaoann09014a/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noaoann09014a" Xcxstatus="in:642762fb975014bc743f177d">
