  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="1.895685156848764" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noirlab2013a&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-18T12:00:00+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="CAS" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="59.30095144517113" Magnitude="0" Name="Portrait of a Starburst Galaxy" Opacity="100" RA="0.3382825829350118" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noirlab2013a/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="1.895685156848764">
    <Description>KPNO captures irregular starburst galaxy IC 10 in glorious detail This striking image from NOIRLab’s Kitt Peak National Observatory (KPNO) presents a portrait of the irregular galaxy IC 10, a disorderly starburst galaxy close to the Milky Way. As well as a population of bright young stars, this irregular galaxy harbors exotic Wolf-Rayet stars and a black hole. IC 10 lies around 2 million light-years from Earth in the direction of the constellation of Cassiopeia. Though this distance seems huge, it puts IC 10 in the ever-growing Local Group of galaxies.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.4744454263103488" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="5.075860218219857" CenterY="59.3008621817521" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Portrait of a Starburst Galaxy" OffsetX="5.79157014539e-05" OffsetY="5.79157014539e-05" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-0.05999999999999904" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="5" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noirlab2013a/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noirlab2013a" Xcxstatus="in:64276304975014bc743f1791">
        <Credits>KPNO/NOIRLab/NSF/AURAData obtained and processed by: P. Massey (Lowell Obs.), G. Jacoby, K. Olsen, &amp;amp; C. Smith (NOAO/AURA/NSF)Image processing: Travis Rector (University of Alaska Anchorage), Mahdi Zamani &amp;amp; Davide de Martin</Credits>
