  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="-2.5545288" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-02202&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:34:06.567881+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="SGR" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-16.1796909561552" Magnitude="0" Name="The Omega Nebula, M17 (NGC 6618)" Opacity="100" RA="18.34983797541266" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-02202/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="1.96853066178134">
    <Description>The Omega Nebula, M17 (NGC6618), as seen by the KPNO 0.9-meter telescope in 1993 (true color CCD image). M17 is a bright nebula with lanes of opaque dust; it is also referred to as the Swan Nebula and the Horseshoe Nebula. M17 is about 5700 light-years away in the direction of Sagittarius, and contains about 800 solar masses of material within its 17-light-year diameter. We also have optical pictures from the KPNO Schmidt and the KPNO Mayall 4-meter telescopes, an emission line image from the KPNO Schmidt, revealing physical conditions in the nebula, and an interesting infrared image from the KPNO 2.1-meter telescope.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="-0.520192" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="275.13897446727253" CenterY="-16.178570302868167" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="The Omega Nebula, M17 (NGC 6618)" OffsetX="0.103748602545" OffsetY="-0.0015843625919999946" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-180.17778159930452" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="4" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-02202/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao-02202" Xcxstatus="in:6427626e975014bc743f1652">
        <Credits>Todd Boroson/NOIRLab/NSF/AURA</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao-02202/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-02202/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="1.97197824" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-m58&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:33:50.667330+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="VIR" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="11.818591357655178" Magnitude="0" Name="M58, NGC 4579" Opacity="100" RA="12.628798806921637" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m58/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="1.97197824">
    <Description>M58 is a spiral galaxy of type SBc in the constellation Virgo, although the bar is not prominent and some have classified it as intermediate between normal and barred spirals. This CCD composite picture was taken in April 1998 at the Kitt Peak 0.9-meter telescope. M58 is a member of the Virgo Cluster of galaxies, which is the dominant cluster in our Local Supercluster and about 60 million light-years away. The Virgo cluster also includes Messier galaxies M49, M59, M60, M61, M84, M85, M86, M87, M88, M89, M90, M91, M98, M99, and M100.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.1933312" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="189.37724353220585" CenterY="11.857863917227748" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="M58, NGC 4579" OffsetX="-0.0530688443985" OffsetY="-0.04040418600799999" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-0.8345558030851804" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="3" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m58/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao-m58" Xcxstatus="in:642762b4975014bc743f16e6">
        <Credits> NOIRLab/NSF/AURA/</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao-m58/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m58/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="1.9740672" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-m14&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:34:15+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="OPH" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-3.245631053573626" Magnitude="0" Name="M14, NGC 6402" Opacity="100" RA="17.626645160150005" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m14/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="1.9740672">
    <Description>M14 is a somewhat looser globular cluster than many, with a very small ellipticity (deviation from being exactly circular on the sky). Appearing in the constellation Ophiuchus, it is about 29000 light-years away and about 72 light-years across. This picture was made from CCD images taken at the KPNO 0.9-meter telescope in April of 1998. Image size 11.6 arc minutes.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.193536" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="264.3307584361532" CenterY="-3.2052346402488614" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="M14, NGC 6402" OffsetX="-0.0683510390448" OffsetY="-0.04167967699350001" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-0.803521066437237" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="3" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m14/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xcxstatus="in:642762a6975014bc743f16c9">
        <Credits>NOIRLab/NSF/AURA</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao-m14/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m14/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="1.9740672" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-m54&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:53:13+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="SGR" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-30.479108445829823" Magnitude="0" Name="M54, NGC 6715" Opacity="100" RA="18.917608155170363" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m54/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="1.9740672">
    <Description>M54 is a globular cluster in the constellation Sagittarius. It is bright but small, and its core is not resolved here. M54 was always thought to be some 60000 light-years away, but there is now a claim that it is actually part of an extragalactic system, a member of the Local Group called the Sagittarius Dwarf, which would move it out to nearly 90000 light-years. This composite was created from images taken in July 1998 at the KPNO 0.9-meter telescope, during the Research Experiences for Undergraduates (REU) program operated at the Kitt Peak National Observatory and supported by the National Science Foundation. Image size 11.6 arc minutes.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.193536" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="283.74501377185135" CenterY="-30.453743309713367" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="M54, NGC 6715" OffsetX="-0.015713185950000003" OffsetY="-0.026463445564499994" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-1.814310587" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="3" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m54/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao-m54" Xcxstatus="in:642762b3975014bc743f16e4">
        <Credits>REU Program/NOIRLab/NSF/AURA</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao-m54/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m54/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="1.9740672" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-m80&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:53:14+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="SCO" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-22.976543625091256" Magnitude="0" Name="M80, NGC 6093" Opacity="100" RA="16.28406172812459" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m80/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="1.9740672">
    <Description>M80, a globular cluster in the constellation Scorpius, is quite distant at over 27000 light-years, and also one of the densest globulars. It contains several hundred thousand stars within a diameter of around seventy light-years. This picture was created from observations with the T2KA CCD camera at the Kitt Peak National Observatory&amp;#x27;s 0.9-meter telescope in March of 1995.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.193536" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="244.1944793846641" CenterY="-23.043904960373283" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="M80, NGC 6093" OffsetX="-0.06269256257670001" OffsetY="0.06568668814050001" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-1.09098264" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="2" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m80/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xcxstatus="in:642762bd975014bc743f16f9">
        <Credits>NOIRLab/NSF/AURA</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao-m80/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m80/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="1.9740672" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-n2261a&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:34:04+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="MON" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="8.793398938743946" Magnitude="0" Name="NGC 2261, Hubble's Variable Nebula" Opacity="100" RA="6.652444343441399" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-n2261a/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="1.9740672">
    <Description>This image of Hubble&amp;#x27;s Variable Nebula in the constellation of Monoceros was taken with the Kitt Peak National Observatory&amp;#x27;s 0.9-meter telescope in March 1995. This deep version of the image shows the faint tail extending down to the south from the nebula. To the north, almost directly above the nebula, is a cluster of faint red dots comprising the Herbig Haro object known as HH39. This object shows strong emission lines, especially those of hydrogen and nitrogen (which provide the red color), and gets its energy from a strong outflow, or jet, emitted by R Monocerotis, which is the young star at the southern end of NGC2261. A shallower realization shows the interior details of the nebula.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.193536" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="99.76870203078937" CenterY="8.788496366283034" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="NGC 2261, Hubble's Variable Nebula" OffsetX="-0.017880684696379993" OffsetY="0.004290254725049998" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-0.9946427685550605" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="3" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-n2261a/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao-n2261a" Xcxstatus="in:642762c7975014bc743f170d">
        <Credits>N.A.Sharp, J.McGaha/NOIRLab/NSF/AURA</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao-n2261a/</CreditsUrl>
