  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Constellation="AQL" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="14.48516456943746" Magnitude="0" Name="W51A - SOFIA" Opacity="100" RA="19.39314183485091" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/packages/2021/02_sofia/w51a_sofia/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="2.407204814395309">
    <Description>Composite image of W51A, the largest star-forming region in our galaxy. Dozens of massive stars that are more than eight times the size of our Sun are forming there. They create intense radiation pressure that has pushed dust out of the star&amp;#x27;s natal cocoon, creating arcs and bubbles that glow brightly at infrared wavelengths of at 37 and 70 microns, shown in green and red in this false color image. Hot gas remains inside these features, which is shown in the 20-micron view in blue. The background star field from Spitzer is shown in white.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.3413340159993348" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="290.573645" CenterY="14.666982" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="W51A - SOFIA" OffsetX="-0.135471020941236" OffsetY="-0.33574100481334573" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-37.92070172353101" Sparse="False" StockSet="False" TileLevels="4" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/packages/2021/02_sofia/w51a_sofia/{1}/{3}/{3}_{2}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xcxstatus="in:647a7ae1d4a2d5e2c5f3e725">
        <Credits>NASA/SOFIA/Wanggi Lim, James De Buizer; NASA/JPL-Caltech</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://www.nasa.gov/feature/sofia-s-infrared-view-of-the-skies</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/packages/2021/02_sofia/w51a_sofia/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="2.40975" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-m90&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:33:53.549980+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="VIR" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="13.18295743253937" Magnitude="0" Name="M90, NGC 4569" Opacity="100" RA="12.613857690690558" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m90/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="2.40975">
    <Description>M90 is a large spiral galaxy of type Sb in the constellation Virgo. Although large, it seems to have a relatively small mass and thus be of low density. The nearby high surface brightness spiral, IC3583, is clearly visible to the north and seems a bit distorted, which led H.C.Arp to include this system in his Atlas of Peculiar Galaxies. M90 is a member of the Virgo Cluster of galaxies, which is the dominant cluster in our Local Supercluster and about 60 million light-years away. This CCD composite picture was taken in April 1998 at the Kitt Peak 0.9-meter telescope.  The Virgo cluster also includes Messier galaxies M49, M58, M59, M60, M61, M84, M85, M86, M87, M88, M89, M91, M98, M99, and M100.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.387072" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="189.19930745351647" CenterY="13.179265044441799" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="M90, NGC 4569" OffsetX="-0.008565402328999999" OffsetY="0.00319979164049999" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-0.7509926731203841" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="4" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m90/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao-m90" Xcxstatus="in:642762c1975014bc743f1701">
