  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="2.3" Classification="Galaxy" Constellation="TRI" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="30.668333333333333" Magnitude="0" Name="The sharpest view ever of the Triangulum Galaxy" Opacity="100" RA="1.5661111111111126" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/packages/2020/07_phat_m33/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="2.5">
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.6413865159570185" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="23.501577996385475" CenterY="30.669082629666004" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="The sharpest view ever of the Triangulum Galaxy" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="0123" Rotation="0.04935307202296003" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="8" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/packages/2020/07_phat_m33/{1}/{3}/{3}_{2}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="esahubble|heic1901a" Xcxstatus="in:649ba2b71e6961df10c19448">
        <Credits>NASA, ESA, and M. Durbin, J. Dalcanton, and B. F. Williams (University of Washington)</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://esahubble.org/images/heic1901a/</CreditsUrl>
        <Description>This gigantic image of the Triangulum Galaxy — also known
        as Messier 33 — is a composite of about 54 different pointings with
        Hubble’s Advanced Camera for Surveys. With a staggering size of
        34,372×19,345 pixels, it is the second-largest image ever released by
        Hubble. It is only dwarfed by the image of the Andromeda Galaxy,
        released in 2015. The mosaic of the Triangulum Galaxy showcases the
        central region of the galaxy and its inner spiral arms. Millions of
        stars, hundreds of star clusters and bright nebulae are visible.</Description>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/packages/2020/07_phat_m33/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="2.5061400000000003" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-m53&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:53:13+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="COM" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="18.167937499754192" Magnitude="0" Name="M53, NGC 5024" Opacity="100" RA="13.21534703551901" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m53/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="2.5061400000000003">
    <Description>M53 is a globular cluster in the constellation Coma Berenices. It is an outlying globular, being about 60000 light-years distant both from us and from the Galactic Center. This composite was created from images taken in July 1997 at the KPNO 0.9-meter telescope, during the Research Experiences for Undergraduates (REU) program operated at the Kitt Peak National Observatory and supported by the National Science Foundation, and again in May 1998. Image size 14.7 arc minutes.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.387072" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="198.28451444367565" CenterY="18.238712882929228" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="M53, NGC 5024" OffsetX="0.05311051045020001" OffsetY="-0.0697965008895" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-1.332264765" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="4" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m53/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xcxstatus="in:642762b2975014bc743f16e3">
        <Credits>REU Program/NOIRLab/NSF/AURA</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao-m53/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m53/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-ngc7129&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:53:16+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="CEP" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="66.11305541386305" Magnitude="0" Name="Star forming region NGC 7129" Opacity="100" RA="21.717802643649406" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-ngc7129/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="2.5063113599999993">
    <Description>NGC 7129 is a star-forming region and reflection nebula in the constellation of Cepheus. It is about 3000 light years away from the Earth. The stars in this nebula are very young, only about a million years old. The red blobs above and below the nebula are Herbig Haro objects, which are the result of jets of gas streaming away from young stars in the nebula.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.5906431999999999" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="325.78352" CenterY="66.05990898" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Star forming region NGC 7129" OffsetX="-0.053164033649999984" OffsetY="0.0065390132465" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-90.14530148877839" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="5" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-ngc7129/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao-ngc7129" Xcxstatus="in:642762d8975014bc743f1733">
