  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-03496&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:33:47.630663+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="HER" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="17.782323144791462" Magnitude="0" Name="Cluster of galaxies in Hercules" Opacity="100" RA="16.08958561655459" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-03496/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="2.5291267200000003">
    <Description>Abell 2151, the Hercules cluster, is a small, irregular cluster, with a core membership of fewer than 100 galaxies, and no strongly dominant central galaxy. It is more than 360 million light-years away and probably contains 1000 billion solar masses of sundry materials. It contains the two peculiar systems Arp 71 (NGC6045) and Arp 272 (NGC6050+IC1179). KPNO 4-meter Mayall telescope, 1974.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.4210688" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="241.3649661" CenterY="17.90155409" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Cluster of galaxies in Hercules" OffsetX="-0.11927419765389997" OffsetY="-0.019906748816200002" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="90.12864954729234" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="5" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-03496/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xcxstatus="in:64276288975014bc743f1688">
        <Credits>NOIRLab/AURA/NSF</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao-03496/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-03496/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;iotw2019a&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-05-06T03:00:00+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="TRI" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="27.371993304793435" Magnitude="0" Name="A (Galactic) Arm’s Length Away" Opacity="100" RA="1.7950624915201876" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/iotw2019a/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="2.5294901205879525">
    <Description>The two galaxies in this image — NGC 672 (top right) and IC 1727 (bottom left) — appear to be so close that they are almost elbowing each other, like playful children. In fact, the galaxies shown in this beautifully detailed image taken at the Kitt Peak National Observatory, a program of NSF’s NOIRLab, are classified as a galactic pair, and the gravity of each galaxy influences the other. The pair are located around 20 million light-years away from Earth and the space between them spans over 80,000 light-years, a seemingly enormous gap. However, from the perspective of the galaxies’ themselves, that separation is not so significant, as it is roughly equivalent to the size of the galaxies themselves.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.2975289618849792" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="26.9258554333" CenterY="27.3719207928" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="A (Galactic) Arm’s Length Away" OffsetX="7.26389067102e-05" OffsetY="7.26389067102e-05" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="90.1" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="4" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/iotw2019a/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|iotw2019a" Xcxstatus="in:6427625d975014bc743f162d">
        <Credits>KPNO/NOIRLab/NSF/AURA Acknowledgements:PI: M T. Patterson (New Mexico State University)Image processing: Travis Rector (University of Alaska Anchorage), Mahdi Zamani &amp;amp; Davide de Martin</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/iotw2019a/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/iotw2019a/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-02286&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:33:58+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="COM" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="25.994933972912328" Magnitude="0" Name="Edge-on spiral NGC 4565" Opacity="100" RA="12.604787017555537" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-02286/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="2.5296">
    <Description>NGC4565 is an edge-on spiral galaxy of type Sb in the constellation Coma Berenices. KPNO 2.1-meter telescope, 1973.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.507904" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="189.0880964" CenterY="25.92940839" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Edge-on spiral NGC 4565" OffsetX="0.012994115875000005" OffsetY="0.06587333496520001" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-1.4264051854307889" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="4" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-02286/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xcxstatus="in:64276276975014bc743f1662">
        <Credits>NOIRLab/NSF/AURA</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao-02286/</CreditsUrl>
