  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="2.7009600000000002" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao0203a&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2002-06-03T00:00:00+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="GEM" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="21.55125663046828" Magnitude="0" Name="Solar composition star cluster" Opacity="100" RA="7.641463154048022" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao0203a/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="2.7009600000000002">
    <Description>New observations of the richly populated star cluster NGC 2420 taken by the refurbished WIYN 0.9-meter telescope at Kitt Peak National Observatory suggest that the cluster contains a multitude of clues about the history and evolution of the Milky Way. NGC 2420 consists of approximately 1,000 stars packed into a roughly spherical volume of space just 30 light-years in diameter. Because all of the stars in a star cluster have similar ages and initial compositions, star clusters can be used as a laboratory to study many different areas of astronomy. Further details of this interesting result can be found in NOAO press release 02-04.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.338944" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="114.61760419557805" CenterY="21.498724161901038" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Solar composition star cluster" OffsetX="-0.003956560140900008" OffsetY="0.0518253517276" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="0.2399902571" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="2" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao0203a/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao0203a" Xcxstatus="in:642762e9975014bc743f1756">
        <Credits>WIYN/NOIRLab/NSF</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao0203a/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao0203a/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="2.7273817798892943" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-lbn438&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:34:05+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="LAC" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="37.78050646515935" Magnitude="0" Name="Dusty Nebula, LBN 438" Opacity="100" RA="22.686510510129665" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-lbn438/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="2.7273817798892943">
    <Description>This image was obtained with the wide-field view of the Mosaic camera on the Mayall 4-meter telescope at Kitt Peak National Observatory. LBN 438 is a dusty nebula within our galaxy. Despite being part of the Lynds Bright Nebula catalog it is very faint. This is the largest fragment of the nebula. There are many smaller fragments outside the field of view. The image was generated with observations in the B (blue), V (green) and I (orange) filters. In this image, North is left, East is down.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.5949108951031807" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="340.29830790503536" CenterY="37.780049684486336" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Dusty Nebula, LBN 438" OffsetX="7.262095887489999e-05" OffsetY="7.262095887489999e-05" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-89.97999999999996" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="5" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-lbn438/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao-lbn438" Xcxstatus="in:6427629d975014bc743f16b7">
