  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao0315b&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2003-11-12T00:00:00+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="TAU" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="23.946461891412564" Magnitude="0" Name="Merope in the Pleiades" Opacity="100" RA="3.772187127347676" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao0315b/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="2.9454335999999994">
    <Description>The star Merope, in the familiar object the Pleiades, in a three-color composite image obtained with the Burrell Schmidt telescope of Case Western Reserve University&amp;#x27;s Warner and Swasey Observatory. The images were taken in January of 1997. The Pleiades, also known as the Seven Sisters and Messier object 45, and Subaru in Japanese (check the logo on the cars of the same name), is one of the more prominent naked eye objects in the sky and, as such, has been known since antiquity, including three mentions in the bible. The Pleiades are embedded in a blue reflection nebula, clearly visible around Merope in this image.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.28876799999999997" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="56.52210684532518" CenterY="23.972536228246753" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Merope in the Pleiades" OffsetX="-0.054524694635199995" OffsetY="-0.0290784121776" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-2.3663404760023505" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="2" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao0315b/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao0315b" Xcxstatus="in:642762ec975014bc743f175d">
        <Credits>NOIRLab/NSF/AURA</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao0315b/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao0315b/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="2.9454336000000003" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-m11&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:34:15+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="SCT" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-6.275291813227073" Magnitude="0" Name="M11, NGC 6705" Opacity="100" RA="18.85138585392197" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m11/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="2.9454336000000003">
    <Description>M11 is one of the richest and most compact open clusters, with nearly 3000 stars packed into only twenty-odd light-years. This color composite was created from CCD images taken at the Burrell Schmidt telescope of the Warner and Swasey Observatory of the Case Western Reserve University, located on Kitt Peak in southwestern Arizona. The contributing images were taken in July 1995 during the Research Experiences for Undergraduates (REU) program operated at the Kitt Peak National Observatory and supported by the National Science Foundation.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.28851396650980493" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="282.8290672346453" CenterY="-6.364101146862854" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="M11, NGC 6705" OffsetX="0.0546071446458" OffsetY="0.09021282667740001" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-2.111135592999992" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="2" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m11/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xcxstatus="in:642762a4975014bc743f16c5">
