  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;eso&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;potw1046a&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2010-11-15T10:00:00+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="DOR" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-64.31858809612856" Magnitude="0" Name="An ancient cluster of stars against a stunning background" Opacity="100" RA="6.50414435282581" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/eso/potw1046a/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="3.195611891802">
    <Description>Among the myriad of stars in this image shines NGC 2257, a collection of cosmic gems bound tightly by gravity. Many billions of years old, but still sparkling brightly, it is an eye-catching astronomical object. NGC 2257 is a globular cluster, the name given to the roughly spherical concentrations of stars that orbit galactic cores, but are often found far out from the centres in the halo areas of galaxies. Globular clusters contain very old stars, being typically over 10 billion years old, and can therefore be used like a &amp;quot;fossil record&amp;quot; to learn more about the Universe’s past. They are densely packed, with tens to hundreds of thousands of stars gathered within a diameter of just a few tens of light-years. NGC 2257 lies on the outskirts of the Large Magellanic Cloud (LMC), a satellite galaxy of our own Milky Way. It is one of 15 very old globular clusters in the LMC. The image is made from data taken with the Wide Field Imager instrument on the 2.2-metre MPG/ESO telescope at La Silla, in B, V and I filters, which are shown here in blue, green and red respectively. The field of view is approximately 20 by 20 arcminutes. These observations were made as part of the ESO Imaging Survey project, which was planned to make public imaging surveys to identify targets for follow-up observations with the Very Large Telescope. Links  ESO Imaging Survey </Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.5413446451199999" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="97.550442" CenterY="-64.32693" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="An ancient cluster of stars against a stunning background" OffsetX="-0.00507911357311057" OffsetY="0.008342270612342502" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="0.009419190300000001" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="5" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/eso/potw1046a/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="eso|potw1046a" Xcxstatus="queue:eso">
        <Credits>ESO</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://www.eso.org/public/images/potw1046a/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/eso/potw1046a/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao0906a&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2009-12-21T00:00:00+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="CEP" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="85.25696841662932" Magnitude="0" Name="NGC 188, Caldwell 1" Opacity="100" RA="0.805673461529693" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao0906a/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="3.1977">
    <Description>NGC188 is an open star cluster in the constellation Cepheus. With a membership of around 120, it may be the oldest known open cluster, although age estimates have varied from as much as 24 billion years (which some may notice is a bit of an anomaly when compared to the current estimates of the age of the Universe), to as `little&amp;#x27; as only five billion years. NGC188 was first noticed by John Herschel before 1833. NGC188&amp;#x27;s most recent claim to fame is as item number 1 in Patrick Moore&amp;#x27;s Caldwell list of challenging objects for observers bored with the Messier catalogue. This image was taken with the WIYN 0.9-meter telescope at the Kitt Peak National Observatory on the night of December 19th 2002 UT. Image size 21.5 arc minutes.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.33792" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="12.55938977" CenterY="85.30479164" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="NGC 188, Caldwell 1" OffsetX="0.03840009681750001" OffsetY="-0.04832207374500001" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="0.9752683484999999" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="3" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao0906a/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao0906a" Xcxstatus="in:642762f2975014bc743f176b">
