  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-hfg1&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:34:03.950100+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="CAS" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="64.92829585804704" Magnitude="0" Name="Planetary Nebula HFG1" Opacity="100" RA="3.061711093438494" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-hfg1/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="3.36362544">
    <Description>This image was obtained with the wide-field view of the Mosaic camera on the Mayall 4-meter telescope at Kitt Peak National Observatory. HFG1 is a planetary nebula in the constellation of Cassiopeia. It was produced by a binary star system (V664 Cas) that is moving rapidly through our Galaxy. The star is moving towards the upper-right of the image. As HFG1 plows through the interstellar medium, a bluish bowshock is produced; and a red trail of gas is left behind in its wake. The image was generated with observations in the Hydrogen alpha (red) and Oxygen [OIII] (blue) filters. In this image, North is down and East is to the right.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.5955583999999999" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="45.74426088" CenterY="64.83636655" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Planetary Nebula HFG1" OffsetX="0.07683346878589999" OffsetY="-0.09207096218229999" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-179.976628" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="5" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-hfg1/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao-hfg1" Xcxstatus="in:64276292975014bc743f169f">
        <Credits>T.A. Rector (University of Alaska Anchorage) and H. Schweiker (WIYN and NOIRLab/NSF/AURA)</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao-hfg1/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-hfg1/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="3.3879971825993285" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-wr157&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:53:16+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="CAS" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="60.4763249383662" Magnitude="0" Name="Sharpless 157 (WR 157)" Opacity="100" RA="23.268030491444776" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-wr157/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="3.3879971825993285">
    <Description>This image of Supernova 2002ic was taken at the Swope 1-meter telescope at Las Campanas Observatory in Chile on January 7, 2003. The supernova is the bright point of light in the center, at the intersection of two background galaxies. The host galaxy of the supernova is extremely faint and is not visible. A strong signal of hydrogen was detected in ground-based spectroscopic observations of the supernova, leading a team of astronomers in Chile and the U.S. to conclude that the source of this Type Ia supernova consisted of a white dwarf that suddenly exploded after gaining hydrogen gas blown off by its gravitational partner, a common asymptotic giant branch (AGB) star three to seven times as massive as the Sun. This is the first unambiguous detection of the progenitor of a Type Ia supernova. Four spectroscopic observations of SN2002ic were made using the Baade 6.5-meter and 2.5-meter Dupont telescopes at Las Campanas between November 2002 and January 2003.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.5954106688946175" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="349.2223071921245" CenterY="60.71543204255272" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Sharpless 157 (WR 157)" OffsetX="0.23885578025127108" OffsetY="0.09973042447352921" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-90.12815728928953" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="5" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-wr157/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao-wr157" Xcxstatus="in:642762e3975014bc743f1749">
