  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;eso&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;potw1805a&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2018-01-29T06:00:00+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="SGR" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-17.682667571498413" Magnitude="0" Name="A diamond in the rough" Opacity="100" RA="19.499979719030144" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/eso/potw1805a/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="3.433122196083459">
    <Description>  Squint or you’ll miss it! At the very centre of this image, taken with the VIMOS instrument attached to ESO’s Very Large Telescope (VLT), you can just about see the faint and fuzzy blue form of a distant galaxy known as the Sagittarius Dwarf Irregular Galaxy.  Discovered in 1977 with the ESO 1-metre Schmidt telescope, situated at ESO’s La Silla observatory, the irregularly shaped — hence the name — dwarf galaxy is approximately 3 million light-years away in the constellation of Sagittarius (The Archer). It is the most distant member of the Local Group of galaxies, of which the Milky Way is a member. Unlike normal galaxies, dwarf galaxies are typically smaller and host a relatively small number of stars. Gravitational tugs from nearby galaxies can often distort the spherical and disc-like shapes of these fragile galaxies — this very process may be responsible for the slightly rectangular shape of this particular dwarf galaxy.Acknowledgement: M. Bellazzini et al. </Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.4664639377948672" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="292.499725668" CenterY="-17.6826960422" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="A diamond in the rough" OffsetX="2.84706993283e-05" OffsetY="2.84706993283e-05" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="0" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="5" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/eso/potw1805a/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="eso|potw1805a" Xcxstatus="queue:eso">
        <Credits>ESO/M. Bellazzini et al.</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://www.eso.org/public/images/potw1805a/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/eso/potw1805a/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Constellation="SER2" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-2.0486929812916372" Magnitude="0" Name="Serpens South Cluster - Magnetic Rivers Feed Young Stars" Opacity="100" RA="18.501283826199135" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/packages/2021/02_sofia/serpens_south_cluster_sofia/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="3.4370309121641083">
    <Description>Composite image of the Serpens South Cluster. Magnetic fields observed by SOFIA are shown as streamlines over an image from the Spitzer Space Telescope. SOFIA&amp;#x27;s data indicate that gravity can overcome some of the strong magnetic fields to deliver material needed for new stars. The magnetic fields have been dragged into alignment with the most powerful flows, as seen in the lower left where the streamlines are following the direction of the narrow, dark filament. This is accelerating the flow of material from interstellar space into the cloud, and fueling the collapse needed to spark star formation</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.42546355915669914" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="277.519257392987" CenterY="-2.04869298129164" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Serpens South Cluster - Magnetic Rivers Feed Young Stars" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="0.0019394764670109038" Sparse="False" StockSet="False" TileLevels="3" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/packages/2021/02_sofia/serpens_south_cluster_sofia/{1}/{3}/{3}_{2}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xcxstatus="in:647a7ae1d4a2d5e2c5f3e724">
        <Credits>NASA/SOFIA/T. Pillai/.Kauffmann; NASA/JPL-Caltech/L. Allen</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://www.nasa.gov/image-feature/magnetic-rivers-feed-young-stars</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/packages/2021/02_sofia/serpens_south_cluster_sofia/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="3.45168" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-m12&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:34:15+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="OPH" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-1.951410306956116" Magnitude="0" Name="M12, NGC 6218" Opacity="100" RA="16.787327002929928" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m12/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="3.45168">
    <Description>M12 is a globular cluster in the constellation Ophiuchus, very similar to its near neighbor M10, although much less centrally concentrated. M12 is around 70 light-years across and some 15000 light-years away. This approximately true-color picture was created from images taken in June 1995 using BVR colors, at the Burrell Schmidt telescope of Case Western Reserve University&amp;#x27;s Warner and Swasey Observatory located on Kitt Peak, near Tucson, Arizona, during the Research Experiences for Undergraduates (REU) program operated at the Kitt Peak National Observatory and supported by the National Science Foundation. Image size 20.3 arc minutes.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.5773346225911049" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="251.90308476789414" CenterY="-1.9838916289956319" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="M12, NGC 6218" OffsetX="-0.0919875729786" OffsetY="-0.03629377133759999" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="177.98249490000003" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="3" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m12/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao-m12" Xcxstatus="in:642762a5975014bc743f16c7">
