  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="3.6543091199999997" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-m13kpno4m&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:34:15+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="HER" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="36.46010943498275" Magnitude="0" Name="M13, NGC 6205" Opacity="100" RA="16.695412369613436" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m13kpno4m/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="3.6543091199999997">
    <Description>This image was obtained with the wide-field view of the Mosaic camera on the Mayall 4-meter telescope at Kitt Peak National Observatory. M13 is a giant globular cluster that consists of several hundred thousand stars. The cluster is about 150 light years in diameter. Like in all globular clusters, the stars are all old. Because the core of the cluster is very bright, many short exposures had to be taken so that it would not be over exposed. The image was generated with observations in B (blue), V (green) and I (red) filters. In this image, North is left, East is down.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.5955583999999999" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="250.54442008047982" CenterY="36.59643256473319" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="M13, NGC 6205" OffsetX="0.13659747471620998" OffsetY="0.0904417019659" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-89.85101876999998" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="5" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m13kpno4m/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao-m13kpno4m" Xcxstatus="in:642762a5975014bc743f16c8">
        <Credits>T.A. Rector (University of Alaska Anchorage) and H. Schweiker (WIYN and NOIRLab/NSF/AURA)</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao-m13kpno4m/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m13kpno4m/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="3.6575159978636713" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-cyg_x1&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:34:13+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="CYG" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="35.23409694903231" Magnitude="0" Name="Cygnus X-1 binary star system" Opacity="100" RA="19.972711462721747" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-cyg_x1/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="3.6575159978636713">
    <Description>This image was obtained with the wide-field view of the Mosaic camera on the Mayall 4-meter telescope at Kitt Peak National Observatory. Cygnus X-1 is a binary star system that contains a black hole. The Cygnus X-1 system looks like a single star in this image. It is the brighter (bottom) of the two stars near the center of the image. Gas from the companion star is being pulled into the black hole. Before it falls in the gas becomes incredibly hot, so hot that it emits X-rays (which is how it was discovered). Some of that gas doesn&amp;#x27;t fall into the black hole but instead is shot out as &amp;#x27;jets&amp;#x27; of hot gas. The jet itself isn&amp;#x27;t visible in this image but you can see the bowshock, the bluish umbrella-like shape at the top center of the image, that is produced when gas in the jet collides with other gas in the nebula. The bright orange gas to the left is the western edge of an emission nebula called Sharpless 101 (Sh2-101). The image was generated with observations in Hydrogen alpha (red) and Sulphur [S II] (blue) filters. In this image, North is up, East is to the left.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.594873908117504" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="299.5907871708564" CenterY="35.23385136769576" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Cygnus X-1 binary star system" OffsetX="7.2616443862e-05" OffsetY="7.2616443862e-05" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="0.08" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="5" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-cyg_x1/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao-cyg_x1" Xcxstatus="in:64276290975014bc743f169a">
        <Credits>T.A. Rector (University of Alaska Anchorage) and H. Schweiker (WIYN and NOIRLab/NSF/AURA)</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao-cyg_x1/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-cyg_x1/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="3.6628200000000004" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-m4&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:34:14+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="SCO" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-26.52438679797293" Magnitude="0" Name="M4, NGC 6121" Opacity="100" RA="16.39306509566361" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m4/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="3.6628200000000004">
    <Description>M4, a globular cluster in the constellation Scorpius, is about 7000 light-years away from us, which makes it a good candidate for being our nearest globular cluster. M4 shows an unusual central bar structure, and would be much brighter if not for considerable intervening interstellar dust, which also accounts for its slightly brown-ish color. This picture was created from observations with the T2KA CCD camera at the Kitt Peak National Observatory&amp;#x27;s 0.9-meter telescope in March of 1995.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.387072" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="245.76513599417115" CenterY="-26.414471461236143" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="M4, NGC 6121" OffsetX="-0.11521753891200001" OffsetY="-0.11250012885300001" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-0.968742145" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="3" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m4/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xcxstatus="in:642762b1975014bc743f16e1">
        <Credits>NOIRLab/NSF/AURA</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao-m4/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m4/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="1" Classification="Unfiltered" Constellation="SGR" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-29.48" Magnitude="0" Name="Comet Hale-Bopp (26 Sep 1995)" Opacity="100" RA="18.2727777777778" Rotation="0" ZoomLevel="3.67333333333333">
