  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="3.8506591199999995" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-ngc3242&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:34:09+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="HYA" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-18.762491042984824" Magnitude="0" Name="Ghost of Jupiter, NGC 3242" Opacity="100" RA="10.40733994825964" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-ngc3242/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="3.8506591199999995">
    <Description>This image was obtained with the wide-field view of the Mosaic Camera on the Mayall 4-meter telescope at Kitt Peak National Observatory on December 15th, 2006. This wide-field image shows the extended cloud of gas near the planetary nebula NGC 3242. Also known as the “Ghost of Jupiter”, NGC 3242 is the bright object just to the upper-right from the center of the image. The nature of the extended nebulosity is not yet known. It may either be a shell of gas that was ejected by NGC 3242 when it was a red giant star. Or it may also be a nearby cloud of interstellar gas that is being illuminated by the planetary nebula. It was observed in the narrowband filters of Oxygen [OIII] (blue) and Hydrogen-Alpha (orange). North is to the left, East is down.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.5906432" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="156.0882427821282" CenterY="-18.72736273389039" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Ghost of Jupiter, NGC 3242" OffsetX="0.020199767872200004" OffsetY="0.03527659281719999" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-269.8917562843174" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="5" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-ngc3242/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao-ngc3242" Xcxstatus="in:642762cf975014bc743f1720">
        <Credits>T.A. Rector/University of Alaska Anchorage, H. Schweiker/WIYN and NOIRLab/NSF/AURA/, L. Frattare and Z. Levay/STScI/AURA/NASA</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao-ngc3242/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-ngc3242/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="3.851519999999999" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-ic1396&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:34:05.086965+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="CEP" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="57.4796600304563" Magnitude="0" Name="IC1396, The Elephant Trunk Nebula" Opacity="100" RA="21.59935097575952" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-ic1396/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="3.851519999999999">
    <Description>This image of the Elephant Trunk Nebula was taken with the Mosaic camera on the WIYN 0.9-meter telescope at Kitt Peak National Observatory near Tucson, Arizona. The Elephant Trunk is a dense, elongated cloud of gas inside a bright cluster of stars known as IC 1396. The trunk conceals many young protostars that are in the process of forming.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.4833279999999999" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="324.0974449" CenterY="57.3330463" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="IC1396, The Elephant Trunk Nebula" OffsetX="-0.14708957841799997" OffsetY="0.05651326103799999" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-89.49180896000016" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="4" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-ic1396/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao-ic1396" Xcxstatus="in:64276295975014bc743f16a4">
        <Credits>T.A. Rector (University of Alaska Anchorage) and WIYN/NOIRLab/NSF/AURA</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao-ic1396/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-ic1396/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="3.8556" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-m110&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:33:55.201102+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="AND" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="41.6876609523128" Magnitude="0" Name="M110, NGC 205" Opacity="100" RA="0.6728671763331775" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m110/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="3.8556">
    <Description>M110 is the second small companion (along with M32) to our sister galaxy, M31, the Andromeda Nebula. It is classified as type E5 or E6, often with the additional &amp;quot;p&amp;quot; for peculiar because of the structure clearly visible in this short exposure image, which is probably due to dust clouds. Because of its small size, it is usually counted as a dwarf spheroidal rather than a normal elliptical. Although it was definitely discovered by Messier and sketched by him, it was not included in his list (perhaps due to carelessness), and was the last object to be added (in 1966). The present picture is a color composite of CCD images from the 0.9-meter telescope of the Kitt Peak National Observatory, near Tucson, Arizona. A deeper exposure showing more of the outer regions of the galaxy appears in this picture of all three of M31, M32 and M110.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.387072" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="10.127278644470726" CenterY="41.72964530423326" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="M110, NGC 205" OffsetX="0.025470997284600005" OffsetY="-0.042405492086999994" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-0.1519116879" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="3" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m110/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao-m110" Xcxstatus="in:642762a4975014bc743f16c6">
        <Credits>NOIRLab/NSF/AURA</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao-m110/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m110/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
