  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="4" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-m33lgs_ubvIha&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:33:48+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="TRI" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="30.6788099837372" Magnitude="0" Name="Panorama of Spiral Galaxy, M33" Opacity="100" RA="1.564935304086898" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m33lgs_ubvIha/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="4">
    <Description>This image was created with data from the Local Group Survey, completed with the Mosaic camera on the Mayall 4-meter telescope at Kitt Peak National Observatory. M31, M33 and our Milky Way are the three largest members of the Local Group of galaxies. M33 is a spiral galaxy that is about ten times smaller than our own. Three &amp;#x27;pointings&amp;#x27; of the camera along the galaxy in five filters were used to make the image. The image was generated with observations with the U (violet), B (blue), V (cyan), I (orange) and H-alpha (red) filters. The five filters show the star colors and HII star forming regions in vivid detail. In this image, North is up, East is to the left.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="1.2276394142063283" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="23.405819844457433" CenterY="30.654480325756303" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Panorama of Spiral Galaxy, M33" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="0123" Rotation="-0.08620000000000039" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="6" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m33lgs_ubvIha/{1}/{3}/{3}_{2}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao-m33lgs_ubvIha" Xcxstatus="in:642762af975014bc743f16dc">
        <Credits>Local Group Survey Team and T.A. Rector (University of Alaska Anchorage)</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao-m33lgs_ubvIha/</CreditsUrl>
