  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="4.933484999999999" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-wlm&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:34:01+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="CET" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-15.4606643419552" Magnitude="0" Name="Wolf–Lundmark–Melotte (WLM) Irregular Galaxy" Opacity="100" RA="0.0328399487791238" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-wlm/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="4">
    <Description>This image was obtained with the wide-field view of the Mosaic II camera on the Blanco 4-meter telescope at Cerro Tololo Interamerican Observatory. Wolf–Lundmark–Melotte (WLM) is an irregular galaxy located on the outer edges of the Local Group. WLM is about ten times smaller than our own galaxy, the Milky Way. This image was made using data from the Local Group Survey. The image was generated with observations in U (violet), B (blue), V (cyan), I (orange) and H-alpha (red) filters. In this image, North is up, East is left.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.6144" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="0.3366721256706939" CenterY="-15.455131998445317" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Wolf–Lundmark–Melotte (WLM) Irregular Galaxy" OffsetX="-0.15188990475" OffsetY="-0.016340077949999997" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="0.10738590000101554" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="5" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-wlm/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xcxstatus="in:642762e1975014bc743f1746">
        <Credits>Local Group Survey Team and T.A. Rector (University of Alaska Anchorage)</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao-wlm/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-wlm/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="6.052108799999999" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao0706b&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2007-05-16T00:00:00+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="PER" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="31.3636453295914" Magnitude="0" Name="NGC 1333" Opacity="100" RA="3.48755684135304" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao0706b/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="4">
    <Description>This image of NGC 1333 was taken using the Mosaic-1 imager and the National Science Foundation’s Mayall 4-meter telescope at Kitt Peak. This star-forming region, located roughly 1,000 light-years from Earth in the direction of the constellation Perseus, harbors hundreds of newly formed stars less than one million years old. Many of these proto-stars exhibit energetic activity such as jets and strong stellar winds.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.5944350754078721" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="52.26528311766921" CenterY="31.283617572915393" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="NGC 1333" OffsetX="-0.023823624410000008" OffsetY="0.08537302330999999" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="0.0958191194475822" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="4" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao0706b/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao0706b" Xcxstatus="in:642762f1975014bc743f1768">
        <Credits>T.A. Rector/University of Alaska Anchorage, H. Schweiker/WIYN and NOIRLab/NSF/AURA</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao0706b/</CreditsUrl>
