  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="5.890867199999999" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-m23&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:34:16+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="SGR" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="-19.00209116683952" Magnitude="0" Name="M23, NGC 6494" Opacity="100" RA="17.94727079044008" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m23/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="5.890867199999999">
    <Description>The irregular star cluster M23, or NGC6494, is a bright, large, rich, rather attractive, cluster in the constellation Sagittarius. Some 2200 light-years away, M23 has a diameter of about 20 light-years (around 30 minutes of arc): this picture is 35 minutes across. M23 is over 200 million years old. As M23 is in the galactic plane, the background stars are increasingly reddened by dust absorption, and this image has several black areas where any distant stars are hidden by the thick dust. The bright star to the NW (upper right) is a foreground object. This true-color picture was created from six images taken in the BVR pass-bands at the Burrell Schmidt telescope of Case Western Reserve University&amp;#x27;s Warner and Swasey Observatory. The Burrell Schmidt is located on Kitt Peak, near Tucson, Arizona. The images were taken in July 1995 during the Research Experiences for Undergraduates (REU) program operated at the Kitt Peak National Observatory and supported by the National Science Foundation.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.5767763769182009" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="269.08028713154795" CenterY="-19.25099651455412" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="M23, NGC 6494" OffsetX="-0.13057022548799996" OffsetY="0.2435498834862" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-2.0471364260000002" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="3" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m23/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xcxstatus="in:642762aa975014bc743f16d2">
        <Credits>N.A.Sharp, REU program/NOIRLab/NSF/AURA</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao-m23/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m23/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="5.890867200000001" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-m106&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:33:54.993253+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="CVN" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="47.313526656166445" Magnitude="0" Name="M106, NGC 4258" Opacity="100" RA="12.316287637182874" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m106/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="5.890867200000001">
    <Description>M106 is a type Sb spiral galaxy in the constellation Canes Venatici. More edge-on than not, at a similar inclination to M31, its prominent dust lanes trace spiral structure back almost to its nucleus. M106 is around 25 million light-years away and may be a member of a loose galaxy grouping which includes M108 and M109, although some have placed it in a different group. A somewhat peculiar spiral, sometimes considered to have hints of a bar, M106 is also a Seyfert galaxy, showing strong emission lines in spectra of its nucleus. This full color image was created from nine images taken in the BVR pass-bands at the Burrell Schmidt telescope of Case Western Reserve University&amp;#x27;s Warner and Swasey Observatory. The Burrell Schmidt is located on Kitt Peak, near Tucson, Arizona. The images were taken in June 1996 during the Research Experiences for Undergraduates (REU) program operated at the Kitt Peak National Observatory and supported by the National Science Foundation. North is at the top with east to the left.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.577536" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="184.99479151168504" CenterY="47.37331134605182" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="M106, NGC 4258" OffsetX="0.1723038448425" OffsetY="-0.05395348249199999" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-2.1870648049968393" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="3" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-m106/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao-m106" Xcxstatus="in:642762a2975014bc743f16c2">
