  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="6.153721200000001" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-ngc7380&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:34:10+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="CEP" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="58.047182286237444" Magnitude="0" Name="Wizard Nebula, NGC 7380" Opacity="100" RA="22.792555530399127" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-ngc7380/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="6.153721200000001">
    <Description>This image was obtained with the wide-field view of the Mosaic camera on the Mayall 4-meter telescope at Kitt Peak National Observatory. Informally known as the “Wizard Nebula”, NGC 7380 is an open cluster of stars still embedded in the nebula out of which it formed. The stars are estimated to be about 4 million years old. The cluster is about 8000 light years away. The image was generated with observations in the Hydrogen alpha (red), Oxygen [OIII] (blue) and Sulfur [SII] (orange) filters. In this image, North is left, East is down.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="1.1894784" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="342.09919229885327" CenterY="58.22575606849877" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Wizard Nebula, NGC 7380" OffsetX="0.1788285125352" OffsetY="0.11125695210299999" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-89.94372082" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="6" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-ngc7380/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao-ngc7380" Xcxstatus="in:642762d9975014bc743f1734">
        <Credits>T.A. Rector (University of Alaska Anchorage) and H. Schweiker (WIYN and NOIRLab/NSF/AURA)</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao-ngc7380/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-ngc7380/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="0" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao0702a&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2007-01-07T00:00:00+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="CAS" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="61.24927446669929" Magnitude="0" Name="The Bubble Nebula (NGC 7635)" Opacity="100" RA="23.34186253851704" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao0702a/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="6.164308799999999">
    <Description>The Bubble Nebula (NGC7635) is one of three shells of gas surrounding the massive star BD+602522, the bright star near the center of the bubble. Energetic radiation from the star ionizes the shell, causing it to glow. About six light-years in diameter, the Bubble Nebula is located in the direction of the constellation Cassiopeia. The magenta wisps near the bottom-right of the image are an unexpected bonus—the wisps are the remnants of a supernova that exploded thousands of years ago. This is the first optical image of the supernova remnant, which was discovered at radio wavelengths by the Canadian Galactic Plane Survey in 2005.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="0.6103039999999998" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="350.1747933" CenterY="61.27983125" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="The Bubble Nebula (NGC 7635)" OffsetX="-0.022626996810999986" OffsetY="0.030482372468999982" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="179.8316954" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="4" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao0702a/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao0702a" Xcxstatus="in:642762f0975014bc743f1765">
        <Credits>T.A. Rector/University of Alaska Anchorage, H. Schweiker/WIYN and NOIRLab/NSF/AURA</Credits>
        <CreditsUrl>https://noirlab.edu/public/images/noao0702a/</CreditsUrl>
        <ThumbnailUrl>http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao0702a/thumb.jpg</ThumbnailUrl>
      </ImageSet>
    </ForegroundImageSet>
  </Place>
  <Place Angle="0" AngularSize="6.164820385080855" Annotation="{&quot;channel&quot;: &quot;noirlab&quot;, &quot;itemid&quot;: &quot;noao-ldn673&quot;, &quot;publishedUTCISO8601&quot;: &quot;2020-06-30T21:34:05+00:00&quot;}" Constellation="AQL" DataSetType="Sky" Dec="11.228623468704242" Magnitude="0" Name="Lynds’ Dark Nebula 673" Opacity="100" RA="19.348679260904163" Rotation="0" Thumbnail="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-ldn673/thumb.jpg" ZoomLevel="6.164820385080855">
    <Description>This image was obtained with the wide-field view of the Mosaic camera on the Mayall 4-meter telescope at Kitt Peak National Observatory. LDN 673 is part of a giant cloud of dust and gas in the constellation of Aquilla. It is fragmented into many pieces, inside of which are forming stars. Some of these stars are illuminating parts of the nebula, such as in the upper-left and upper-right corners. The image was generated with observations in B (blue), V (cyan), I (orange) and H-alpha (red) filters. In this image, North is left, East is down.</Description>
    <ForegroundImageSet>
      <ImageSet BandPass="Visible" BaseDegreesPerTile="1.1906208779698175" BaseTileLevel="0" BottomsUp="False" CenterX="290.20692346673485" CenterY="11.206825557276822" DataSetType="Sky" ElevationModel="False" FileType=".png" Generic="False" Name="Lynds’ Dark Nebula 673" OffsetX="-0.02175964600688506" OffsetY="-0.02302612113109316" Projection="Tan" QuadTreeMap="" Rotation="-89.82753481548649" Sparse="True" StockSet="False" TileLevels="6" Url="http://data1.wwtassets.org/feeds/noirlab/noao-ldn673/L{1}X{2}Y{3}.png" WidthFactor="2" Xastropix_ids="noirlab|noao-ldn673" Xcxstatus="in:6427629e975014bc743f16b9">
        <Credits>T.A. Rector (University of Alaska Anchorage) and H. Schweiker (WIYN and NOIRLab/NSF/AURA)</Credits>
